[{:db/id 17592186164192 :answer-field/correct 17592186164193 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164189 :answer-field/correct 17592186164191 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164195 :answer-field/correct 17592186164198 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-331517"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-332705/selection/761ca41454cf386491acdc3fc2ee895be91d4e3f5dcac1c2b553f30766ff20e5" :answer/id #uuid "35878faf-2f32-58fd-8041-eb3a7c9b9dd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332705/selection/847a8ae782d148f207d5bf5395aa2cc5a09c4623d3e8d2b5f32f09893b968ae5" :answer/id #uuid "75003487-d53b-5f98-bb5a-3a4e0515ea9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332705/selection/f82f5c28de611a823b80854c83185cc7d826140ce18f01003d08833f64538d98" :answer/id #uuid "9a88b7eb-b01e-535d-b28c-b29d8adb3422" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332705/selection/2a63a57b890157b91053d0c5d6028b72b938b85994472af20d85b57adbadec50" :answer/id #uuid "9ca7fa45-203d-57cc-9e33-c06576540153" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332705/selection/7574355d18b0fbc7385f9f77263e77af4c273f70587a1df22ec8759b58bbbdc2" :answer/id #uuid "b843cd9a-4d95-5519-9b64-6cd703d2b1f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332705/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0edb3105-eec6-57bf-a132-e339c8cc4c84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332705/selection/761ca41454cf386491acdc3fc2ee895be91d4e3f5dcac1c2b553f30766ff20e5" :answer-field/choices ["q-332705/selection/761ca41454cf386491acdc3fc2ee895be91d4e3f5dcac1c2b553f30766ff20e5" "q-332705/selection/847a8ae782d148f207d5bf5395aa2cc5a09c4623d3e8d2b5f32f09893b968ae5" "q-332705/selection/f82f5c28de611a823b80854c83185cc7d826140ce18f01003d08833f64538d98" "q-332705/selection/2a63a57b890157b91053d0c5d6028b72b938b85994472af20d85b57adbadec50" "q-332705/selection/7574355d18b0fbc7385f9f77263e77af4c273f70587a1df22ec8759b58bbbdc2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332705" :question/id #uuid "1ced5b6b-97cb-5a57-b3b4-0d4662b19d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332705" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/98ffa8fec669ce09d22b5b904b710632314f7b22e0eb5098719c41147c186975.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332705/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332705"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332710/selection/12816ca5e71fb54ad4e2b29aa9a94a73282fdaf2cb87aa096e4f04d16dc834e9" :answer/id #uuid "d546ab91-83e1-5427-8e26-0eb4d4786009" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332710/selection/403a2f48f9a9c1b7ed1667f61f15b06676df2fc7bc3e6247557393dcde419e86" :answer/id #uuid "f07067d0-9ecc-5660-a221-4d36dfec149a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332710/selection/13f0dda84931f743654b2a659fd652860ca0fa579f2e4b805cebb9267deae4c4" :answer/id #uuid "09f62c1c-c516-5303-af46-a437453e5376" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332710/selection/4d2b353f7db425cd45d9926fd1e098803ff480133b397783c4cc02cb659def84" :answer/id #uuid "2fd7f1d3-13e4-54f4-a07e-c38ea3bfbb18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332710/selection/1a9c5f12061d24ef77cc119cdf07f6c57b626aa47887238fcd6ec15390c3fdc7" :answer/id #uuid "5b13e5e4-d888-51fa-8949-3ccdeabdb0ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332710/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "11e8cfc5-69ae-51bf-bca3-4ed348ffe8c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332710/selection/12816ca5e71fb54ad4e2b29aa9a94a73282fdaf2cb87aa096e4f04d16dc834e9" :answer-field/choices ["q-332710/selection/12816ca5e71fb54ad4e2b29aa9a94a73282fdaf2cb87aa096e4f04d16dc834e9" "q-332710/selection/403a2f48f9a9c1b7ed1667f61f15b06676df2fc7bc3e6247557393dcde419e86" "q-332710/selection/13f0dda84931f743654b2a659fd652860ca0fa579f2e4b805cebb9267deae4c4" "q-332710/selection/4d2b353f7db425cd45d9926fd1e098803ff480133b397783c4cc02cb659def84" "q-332710/selection/1a9c5f12061d24ef77cc119cdf07f6c57b626aa47887238fcd6ec15390c3fdc7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332710" :question/id #uuid "781b48d3-e667-556c-9dcb-a64c772b6461" :question/math-academy-id "q-332710" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/83a565accc834d7c16091de22eb43ba80c0ce9c4290f567d1e0ccc009beca565.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332710/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332710"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333193/selection/221699cbc5f7c6fa6b9593ae4949dc7c5007e9dcd2e12bcb17da8a95e16cea6e" :answer/id #uuid "07b7a00e-07eb-5c01-a60e-9fce25b101e4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/e8e9a97ed196b5677e3c2ea6be32f4a1ab04f7721b3d9c41e4db284d69e6805a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333193/selection/59d450903b4992159fb3f52e589d7fece199915c94c42567298adaab5812a0d1" :answer/id #uuid "d4110cc0-3c55-5ad4-93b6-bc59140f4ded" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/85cb5c01c72b8b903cc0b5fc8c593c0b6e96550380e6bc7af55d5db5b5f40d4e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333193/selection/1a73169f02131aa3141bf548b581829fb724461e67b9dbede167a40157a306a6" :answer/id #uuid "158c7765-5b2c-57ae-be67-ab360e70b06c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/0c70e2c185d054b8cc62411736ecec2e60a716d265819a82a171076d999ef4e6.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333193/selection/1082ea7466754729b7fbf53ce08e4fb60a4c1262adb7da8b66cf36f3c997b21f" :answer/id #uuid "b19c52ef-634a-51aa-9a19-9044a1c96217" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/b414b76f1fe20b5238e75281e57bb851c8e67777e225d7610631dab1b4921b82.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333193/selection/3801a76e10b0c07889abecad6a74cd722bd1cf71e195d44188eaa8404f3a3bb9" :answer/id #uuid "130e6680-2ac5-572b-ab0a-ebbcc4f22140" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/d19a6d30c12f5d966578cd950db1f16a9bc309f3a24a8e2480e52edca1281f75.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333193/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f3b3538-d107-54da-8db2-c4f406514268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333193/selection/221699cbc5f7c6fa6b9593ae4949dc7c5007e9dcd2e12bcb17da8a95e16cea6e" :answer-field/choices ["q-333193/selection/221699cbc5f7c6fa6b9593ae4949dc7c5007e9dcd2e12bcb17da8a95e16cea6e" "q-333193/selection/59d450903b4992159fb3f52e589d7fece199915c94c42567298adaab5812a0d1" "q-333193/selection/1a73169f02131aa3141bf548b581829fb724461e67b9dbede167a40157a306a6" "q-333193/selection/1082ea7466754729b7fbf53ce08e4fb60a4c1262adb7da8b66cf36f3c997b21f" "q-333193/selection/3801a76e10b0c07889abecad6a74cd722bd1cf71e195d44188eaa8404f3a3bb9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333193" :question/id #uuid "619ed97d-e84b-53da-afc0-cbcda9b4c479" :question/math-academy-id "q-333193" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/3fdbeba46a0f4640e909b0ba489c1aec1fa87a6aa7740f19d08f274b7351d11b.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-3{c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/4193300af5a8950ce70e2c469812424235665faac279dac0c89289a0307ab46a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333193/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333193"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164443 :answer-field/correct 17592186164444 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164450 :answer-field/correct 17592186164452 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164446 :answer-field/correct 17592186164449 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333216"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076662/assets/19cd34ffc84e4a904d87cdfe0d8b6721861a0a39025484946dbe471a5ea4ad87.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
